ce:section.ce:para:
A key methodological issue for the planned comparative analyses was whether these two forms of escapism could be operationalized as empirically distinguishable constructs. To address this, we compared a two-factor confirmatory model, in which the four world-focused items loaded on one latent factor and the four self-focused items loaded on a second latent factor, with a one-factor alternative in which all eight items loaded on a single general escapism factor. The two-factor model showed better fit than the one-factor model, χ2(19) = 48.37, CFI = .996, TLI = .993, RMSEA = .105, SRMR = .067, compared with χ2(20) = 100.13, CFI = .988, TLI = .983, RMSEA = .169, SRMR = .098 for the one-factor solution. Thus, although the fit of the two-factor model was not uniformly ideal, particularly with respect to RMSEA, it was clearly superior to the one-factor alternative and provided preliminary support for distinguishing escapism from the world and escapism from the self. However, the RMSEA value should be interpreted cautiously given known limitations of this index in small-df CFA models (Kenny et al., 2015). All items showed substantial loadings on their intended factors (see Table 1
                         for item wording and standardized loadings). At the same time, the two latent factors were strongly correlated (see Table 2
                        ), indicating that they should be interpreted as closely related rather than independent dimensions. Internal consistency was high for escapism from the world (Cronbach's α = .92) and satisfactory for escapism from the self (Cronbach's α = .81). Detailed psychometric results, including a full comparison of the one-factor and two-factor models, are reported in the Supplementary Materials.
